  <!-- LOCAL KNOWLEDGE SECTION -->
  <section class="pf-section" style="background:#f8fafc;">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="pf-section-label">OLD SWAN LOCAL KNOWLEDGE</span>
      <h2>Why L13 Plumbing Is Its Own Thing</h2>

      <div class="pf-card pf-card-left-orange" style="margin-top:1.5rem;">
        <p>Old Swan is named after the Three Swans pub. The pub served the pack-horse route along Prescot Lane in the 1700s. That route is now Prescot Road, the A57, running straight through the heart of L13.</p>

        <p>The Swan roundabout is the centre. Old Swan library (Victorian black-and-white, opened 1913) sits just off it. St Anne's Church on Prescot Road carries stained glass windows in memory of Ken Dodd, endowed by Lady Dodd in 2022. Britain's first street tramway opened here in 1861. The area has real history.</p>

        <p>The housing tells the same story. Nearly all Victorian terraces from the 1880s and 1900s. Built when the tramway brought workers out from the city centre. The streets running off Prescot Road and Derby Road are mostly intact 130 years on.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="pf-card pf-card-left-orange" style="margin-top:1rem;">
        <p>What's changed is the tenure. 45% of L13 homes are now private rentals. Only 47% are owner-occupied. The biggest age group is 20 to 39. Lots of young professionals, NHS staff and students. The rental market is one of the strongest in Liverpool.</p>

        <p>The plumbing reality is the patchwork. Victorian lead pipework meets 1970s compression fittings meets 2010s plastic push-fit. Connections between different materials fail at the join. A boiler from 2022 fitted to a system from 1995. We work it all out before we quote.</p>

        <p>Alder Hey Hospital is just north. The major extension opens in 2026. That's another wave of NHS rental demand in L13. We cover them all at the same fixed rates.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>


